1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,143
Voicemail catches the calls nobody can answer.

2
00:00:04,143 --> 00:00:08,615
This video shows why it matters, where a message goes, how to record

3
00:00:08,615 --> 00:00:13,027
your greeting, how to listen to your messages, and how a team works

4
00:00:13,027 --> 00:00:14,780
a shared voicemail box.

5
00:00:19,380 --> 00:00:24,180
Agents step away, queues fill up, and calls arrive after hours.

6
00:00:24,180 --> 00:00:29,120
A caller who reaches nobody and hears silence rarely calls again.

7
00:00:29,870 --> 00:00:33,273
A caller who can leave a message is still a customer.

8
00:00:33,273 --> 00:00:37,310
But a message that sits unheard is no better than a missed call.

9
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:40,114
So voicemail has two sides:

10
00:00:40,114 --> 00:00:44,034
a greeting that tells the caller what to do, and a way to

11
00:00:44,034 --> 00:00:47,830
make sure someone listens and calls back, exactly once.

12
00:00:52,030 --> 00:00:56,726
Voicemail goes to one of two places, chosen on the inbound entry point.

13
00:00:56,726 --> 00:01:01,970
The first is your own inbox, which you play from the soft phone's Voicemail tab.

14
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:07,239
The second is a queue's shared voicemail box, which the whole team works

15
00:01:07,239 --> 00:01:10,020
from Interaction Center, Shared voicemail.

16
00:01:14,197 --> 00:01:17,357
Open Interaction Center, My voicemail greeting.

17
00:01:17,357 --> 00:01:21,709
Your greeting is what callers hear before they leave you a message.

18
00:01:21,709 --> 00:01:25,167
If you do not set one, the default greeting is used.

19
00:01:25,695 --> 00:01:28,559
Record with microphone is the default.

20
00:01:28,559 --> 00:01:33,051
Click Record, speak, then click Stop, which is the same button.

21
00:01:33,051 --> 00:01:38,845
If your browser cannot record, the page says so, and you can upload a file instead.

22
00:01:39,310 --> 00:01:43,525
Choose Upload a file to pick an audio file:

23
00:01:43,525 --> 00:01:45,675
M.P.3, wave, O.G.G.

24
00:01:45,675 --> 00:01:49,030
or web M, up to twenty megabytes.

25
00:01:49,493 --> 00:01:52,538
Listen to the Preview, and click Save greeting.

26
00:01:52,538 --> 00:01:57,250
The platform keeps the master copy, and sends it to whichever phone provider

27
00:01:57,250 --> 00:01:58,973
a caller reaches you on.

28
00:01:59,424 --> 00:02:01,908
Remove greeting goes back to the default.

29
00:02:01,908 --> 00:02:05,871
When you have no greeting of your own, callers hear the entry point's

30
00:02:05,871 --> 00:02:10,204
Default voicemail greeting, and when that is empty too, a built-in greeting.

31
00:02:14,410 --> 00:02:17,873
Open the soft phone and choose the Voicemail tab.

32
00:02:17,873 --> 00:02:21,652
A badge on the tab shows how many messages are unread.

33
00:02:21,652 --> 00:02:25,430
Opening the tab marks them read, and clears the badge.

34
00:02:25,912 --> 00:02:29,086
Each message shows the caller's number and the time.

35
00:02:29,086 --> 00:02:31,110
Here there are no messages yet.

36
00:02:31,110 --> 00:02:34,940
Play a message with the play button on its row; the player keeps

37
00:02:34,940 --> 00:02:37,292
the caller's number and time in view.

38
00:02:37,292 --> 00:02:41,012
Every playback is recorded in the audit trail under your name.

39
00:02:41,465 --> 00:02:44,866
When there are messages, you can delete them:

40
00:02:44,866 --> 00:02:48,935
tick one or more rows, or select all, and click Delete.

41
00:02:48,935 --> 00:02:52,936
Deleting removes the message and erases its recording.

42
00:02:52,936 --> 00:02:57,405
A message under legal hold must be kept, and the row says so.

43
00:03:01,605 --> 00:03:06,302
You can play and delete exactly the voicemails in your own Voicemail tab.

44
00:03:06,302 --> 00:03:10,345
Nobody can play or delete a message in another person's inbox.

45
00:03:11,095 --> 00:03:14,273
A call to you that you did not answer goes to your inbox,

46
00:03:14,273 --> 00:03:17,955
and so does a message when you clicked Voicemail on a ringing call.

47
00:03:18,705 --> 00:03:22,105
A queue call offered to you that rang out also goes to you,

48
00:03:22,105 --> 00:03:26,289
when the queue's Unanswered offer action is Send to voicemail, and so does

49
00:03:26,289 --> 00:03:30,316
a queue call that reached voicemail after its maximum wait, as the last

50
00:03:30,316 --> 00:03:31,885
agent it was offered to.

51
00:03:32,635 --> 00:03:37,269
Any other queue call that reached voicemail goes to the agent set as

52
00:03:37,269 --> 00:03:39,775
the entry point's Voicemail inbox.

53
00:03:39,775 --> 00:03:44,284
With nobody set, the message is recorded but is in nobody's inbox.

54
00:03:44,284 --> 00:03:49,795
To let a supervisor hear a queue line, set the Voicemail inbox to that supervisor.

55
00:03:53,997 --> 00:03:59,018
A queue's shared voicemail box lets a team share the messages left on a line:

56
00:03:59,018 --> 00:04:03,435
anyone can claim a message, call back, and mark it done, so nothing

57
00:04:03,435 --> 00:04:06,883
is missed and nobody calls the same customer twice.

58
00:04:06,883 --> 00:04:09,787
Open Interaction Center, Shared voicemail.

59
00:04:10,284 --> 00:04:15,301
Filter by queue, which is All my queues by default, and by status:

60
00:04:15,301 --> 00:04:19,830
Open, which is new and claimed, New, Claimed, Done, or All.

61
00:04:19,830 --> 00:04:21,502
Then click Filter.

62
00:04:21,502 --> 00:04:25,404
You see the queues on your own entitlement record.

63
00:04:27,004 --> 00:04:30,700
Play a message, then click I'll handle this to claim it, so the

64
00:04:30,700 --> 00:04:32,896
rest of the team knows it is yours.

65
00:04:32,896 --> 00:04:34,771
It then shows Claimed by you.

66
00:04:34,771 --> 00:04:38,574
Click Call back to call the caller back from your own soft phone.

67
00:04:39,324 --> 00:04:43,596
When you are finished, click Mark done and add an optional note.

68
00:04:43,596 --> 00:04:48,784
Return to queue releases your claim, and Reopen brings back a finished message.

69
00:04:49,534 --> 00:04:54,226
Take over claims a message someone else is holding, and Delete removes a

70
00:04:54,226 --> 00:04:56,572
message and erases its recording.

71
00:04:56,572 --> 00:05:02,286
Both need the permission to manage shared queue voicemail, which the Supervisor role has.

72
00:05:02,286 --> 00:05:05,354
A message under legal hold cannot be deleted.

73
00:05:09,554 --> 00:05:13,692
An agent who is often away records a greeting that gives an email

74
00:05:13,692 --> 00:05:15,674
address and a callback time.

75
00:05:16,424 --> 00:05:21,401
A support queue sends after-hours callers to its shared voicemail box, and the

76
00:05:21,401 --> 00:05:24,364
morning team claims and clears the messages.

77
00:05:25,114 --> 00:05:29,573
A supervisor is set as the entry point's voicemail inbox for a busy

78
00:05:29,573 --> 00:05:33,054
line, so every message that nobody caught is heard.

79
00:05:33,804 --> 00:05:37,965
A team lead filters the shared box by Open, and checks before lunch

80
00:05:37,965 --> 00:05:40,644
that nothing is waiting without an owner.

81
00:05:42,644 --> 00:05:46,903
Record a greeting, listen to your own inbox, and let the team claim,

82
00:05:46,903 --> 00:05:51,278
call back and mark done in the shared box, so no message goes unheard.

83
00:05:51,278 --> 00:05:52,774
Thanks for watching.

